    content: "Logical operators combine multiple conditions.\n\nOperators:\n&&   Logical AND: both conditions must be true\n||   Logical OR: at least one condition must be true\n!    Logical NOT: inverts true/false\n\nExamples:\nint age = 25;\nbool hasID = true;\n\n// AND: both must be true\nif (age >= 18 && hasID) {\n    cout << \"Enter\";\n}\n\n// OR: at least one must be true\nif (age < 13 || age > 65) {\n    cout << \"Discount\";\n}\n\n// NOT: inverts\nif (!hasID) {\n    cout << \"Need ID\";\n}\n\nCombining Multiple Operators:\nif (age >= 18 && age <= 65 && hasID) {\n    cout << \"Standard entry\";\n}\n\nShort-Circuit Evaluation:\n- && stops at first false\n- || stops at first true\n\nExample:\nif (ptr != nullptr && ptr->value > 0) {\n    // If ptr is null, second condition is NOT evaluated\n    // This prevents null pointer dereference\n}\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Using & instead of &&\nif (x & 2)   // Bitwise AND (checks if bit 1 is set)\nif (x && 2)  // Logical AND (true if x is non-zero)\n\n- Confusing && and ||\n// \"At least 18 AND have ID\" (not OR)\nif (age >= 18 && hasID) { ... }",
    difficulty: "beginner",
    estimatedMinutes: 10,
    topic: "conditionals",
