    id: "switch-statement",
    subject: "cpp",
    title: "switch",
    content: "The switch statement is an alternative to multiple if-else when comparing one value against many constants.\n\nBasic Syntax:\nint day = 3;\nswitch (day) {\n    case 1:\n        cout << \"Monday\";\n        break;\n    case 2:\n        cout << \"Tuesday\";\n        break;\n    case 3:\n        cout << \"Wednesday\";\n        break;\n    default:\n        cout << \"Other day\";\n        break;\n}\nOutput: Wednesday\n\nThe break Statement:\nWithout break, execution \"falls through\" to the next case:\nint x = 1;\nswitch (x) {\n    case 1: cout << \"one \";    // No break!\n    case 2: cout << \"two \";    // No break!\n    case 3: cout << \"three \";  // No break!\n}\nOutput: one two three\n\nFall-Through Can Be Useful:\nswitch (month) {\n    case 2:\n        days = 28;\n        break;\n    case 4: case 6: case 9: case 11:\n        days = 30;\n        break;\n    default:\n        days = 31;\n        break;\n}\n\nswitch vs if-else:\nUse switch when:\n- Comparing one variable against multiple constants\n- Cases are integer, char, or enum values\n\nUse if-else when:\n- Conditions are ranges (x > 10)\n- Conditions involve different variables\n- Floating-point comparisons",
