    title: "while Loop",
    content: "The while loop repeats as long as a condition is true.\n\nSyntax:\nwhile (condition) {\n    // body\n}\n\nExamples:\n// Count from 0 to 4\nint i = 0;\nwhile (i < 5) {\n    cout << i << \" \";\n    i++;\n}\nOutput: 0 1 2 3 4\n\n// Sum until user enters 0\nint sum = 0, num;\ncout << \"Enter numbers (0 to stop): \";\ncin >> num;\nwhile (num != 0) {\n    sum += num;\n    cin >> num;\n}\ncout << \"Sum: \" << sum;\n\n// Input validation\nint age;\ncout << \"Enter age (1-120): \";\ncin >> age;\nwhile (age < 1 || age > 120) {\n    cout << \"Invalid. Try again: \";\n    cin >> age;\n}\n\nwhile vs for:\n- Use for when you know the number of iterations\n- Use while when the number of iterations is unknown\n- Both can be used interchangeably\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Forgetting to update the variable\nint i = 0;\nwhile (i < 5) {\n    cout << i;\n    // Forgot i++ -> infinite loop!\n}\n\n- Infinite loop with condition always true\nwhile (true) { ... }  // Use break to exit",
