    id: "function-parameters",
    subject: "cpp",
    title: "Function Parameters",
    content: "Parameters are the values passed to a function.\n\nPass by Value (Default):\nvoid increment(int x) {\n    x++;  // Only modifies local copy\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    increment(a);\n    cout << a;  // Still 5 (original unchanged)\n}\n\nPass by Reference (Use &):\nvoid increment(int& x) {\n    x++;  // Modifies original variable\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    increment(a);\n    cout << a;  // Now 6 (original modified)\n}\n\nPass by Const Reference (Read-Only, Efficient):\nvoid print(const string& s) {\n    cout << s;\n    // s = \"new\";  // Error: const prevents modification\n}\n\nUse const& for:\n- Large objects (avoids copying)\n- When you don't need to modify the parameter\n- String parameters (string copy is expensive)\n\nDefault Parameters:\nvoid greet(string name = \"Guest\") {\n    cout << \"Hello, \" << name;\n}\n\ngreet(\"Alice\");  // Hello, Alice\ngreet();          // Hello, Guest\n\nRule: Default parameters must be at the end of the parameter list.\nvoid foo(int a = 1, int b);  // Error!\nvoid foo(int a, int b = 2);  // OK",
