    content: "Arrays use 0-based indexing: the first element is at index 0.\n\nint arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n\nIndex:    0    1    2    3    4\nElement: 10   20   30   40   50\n\nAccessing by Index:\narr[0] = 10  (first)\narr[1] = 20\narr[2] = 30\narr[3] = 40\narr[4] = 50  (last)\n\nOff-by-One Error:\nfor (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {  // WRONG: i <= 5 goes out of bounds\n    cout << arr[i];\n}\n\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {  // CORRECT: i < 5 stays in bounds\n    cout << arr[i];\n}\n\nThe Golden Rule:\nFor array of size N, valid indices are 0 to N-1.\nArray of size 5: valid indices are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\n\nArray Traversal:\nint arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\n\n// Method 1: Index-based\nfor (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\n    cout << arr[i] << \" \";\n}\n\n// Method 2: Range-based (C++11)\nfor (int x : arr) {\n    cout << x << \" \";\n}\n\n// Method 3: Using pointers\nfor (int* p = arr; p < arr + 5; p++) {\n    cout << *p << \" \";\n}\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Using <= instead of < in loop condition\n- Using unsigned types for loop counters (underflow risk)\n- Accessing arr[-1] (wraps around to huge index)",
