    id: "getline",
    subject: "cpp",
    title: "getline()",
    content: "getline() reads an entire line of input, including spaces.\n\nWhy getline()?\ncin >> name;  // Reads only up to the first space\n// Input: \"John Smith\"\n// name gets \"John\" (not \"John Smith\")\n\nUsing getline:\nstring fullName;\ncout << \"Enter name: \";\ngetline(cin, fullName);\n// Input: \"John Smith\"\n// fullName gets \"John Smith\"\n\ngetline with Delimiter:\nstring line;\ngetline(cin, line, ',');  // Read until comma\n// Input: \"apple,banana,cherry\"\n// line gets \"apple\"\n\nMixing cin >> and getline:\nint age;\nstring name;\ncout << \"Age: \";\ncin >> age;\ncout << \"Name: \";\ngetline(cin, name);  // PROBLEM: reads the leftover newline!\n\n// Fix: clear the newline buffer\ncin >> age;\ncin.ignore();  // Discard the newline\ngetline(cin, name);  // Now works correctly\n\nReading Multiple Lines:\nstring line;\nwhile (getline(cin, line)) {\n    if (line.empty()) break;  // Stop on empty line\n    cout << \"Read: \" << line << endl;\n}\n\nExample: Read a Sentence and Count Words:\nstring sentence;\ngetline(cin, sentence);\n\nint wordCount = 0;\nbool inWord = false;\nfor (char c : sentence) {\n    if (c == ' ') {\n        inWord = false;\n    } else if (!inWord) {\n        wordCount++;\n        inWord = true;\n    }\n}\ncout << \"Words: \" << wordCount;",
