    subject: "cpp",
    title: "Pointers",
    content: "A pointer stores the memory address of another variable.\n\nDeclaring Pointers:\nint x = 10;\nint* p = &x;  // p stores the address of x\n\nThe & Operator (Address-Of):\nint x = 10;\nint* p = &x;  // p = 0x7ffd5e8a3c4c (some address)\n\nThe * Operator (Dereference):\ncout << *p;    // 10 (go to address, read value)\n*p = 20;       // x is now 20\n\nMemory Visualization:\nint x = 10;    // Variable x, value 10, address 0x100\nint* p = &x;   // Pointer p, value 0x100, address 0x200\n\nMemory:\nAddress  Value\n0x100    10       (x)\n0x200    0x100    (p)\n\nNull Pointer:\nint* p = nullptr;  // Points to nothing\nif (p != nullptr) {\n    cout << *p;  // Only dereference non-null pointers\n}\n\nPointer Arithmetic:\nint arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};\nint* p = arr;      // p points to arr[0]\n*(p + 1);          // 20 (arr[1])\n*(p + 2);          // 30 (arr[2])\np++;                // p now points to arr[1]\n\nCommon Mistakes:\n- Dereferencing null pointer: *nullptr (crash)\n- Dangling pointer: p points to freed memory\n- Uninitialized pointer: int* p; *p = 5; (wild pointer)",
    difficulty: "intermediate",
